Here's the version 0.2 of the Bard patch. It is not complete yet (not
much time to work on this), and to be honest, I don't know exactly if it
really adds to the gameplay or if it is only a bunch of ideas wrapped
together.

Some months ago, when I posted here in r.g.r.n about the idea of
including a bard in nethack, lots of people posted more ideas. At the
beginning I simply started to code, throwing all the ideas into the
patch. At the end of this, I have the feel that the thing was not
right... the patch was a bunch of ideas wrapped together... so I decided
to start over again. I've made the ability to play spells as songs
(named just 'music') an skill. At first I was reluctant to do this, but
it makes more sense now. The music system works this way: if a player
has the music skill, and a suitable instrument, s|he can try to 'cast'
that spell as a song. For (almost) each spell there is a song, with
similar, but not equal, effects of the spell. I tried to make it
different enough, because if it was not it will not make sense in
including the music ability at all -- the bard would just be a lousy
spellcaster with the inconvenient of having to have a musical instrument
to 'cast' spells.

The patch and the documentation are respectively at the addresses below:
http://bertelli.endofinternet.net/stuffs/bard-nh342.patch
http://bertelli.endofinternet.net/stuffs/bard.html

Here are some more details:
I've given the music skill to bards up to expert level; monks, priests,
tourists and elves have it up to basic. I think giving it to elves is a
bit unbalancing, since they're already good spellcasters, but it seems
the right thing to do, since elves are fond of music (and elven priests
even start the game with a random instrument).

I tried to balance the need of musical instruments making some of them
more suitable to certain spells than others. For example: harps are nice
to play divination and enchantment songs, but cannot play attack songs
at all, while horns are good to play attack songs but cannot play
enchantment ones. There are some songs which need a specific instrument
to be played; an example is the "flaming sphere" song, which requires
the fire horn to be played (as well as the knowledge of the fireball
spell). The bard doesn't need to know any spells to play songs (but it
helps to increase the success rate).

The idea for the songs I got from the AD&D bard (I googled some
information, I never played RPGs myself). I tried to make the songs
effects similar to the spells, but adding some difference; several songs
are versions of the spells with an 'area-of-effect'. An example: the
healing spell can be played as the "Heal allies" song, which heal nearby
pets (but not the player). The sleep spell can be played so nearby
monsters will be put to sleep (if they not resist), instead of releasing
a ray of sleep. And so on.

The success rate are calculated based on dexterity (and charisma, for
enchantment songs). I'm not very good at doing these kind of functions;
I copied the one used for spells (percent_success()) and tweaked it a
lot.

The quest nemesis is Hades, the leader is Orpheus, and the artifact is
the Lyre of Harmony. The Lyre has better success rates for all spell
schools, and provides MR. I'm not yet sure about the #invoke effect.

As I've said, I'm not too sure if the bard patch really adds new things
to gameplay or if it is just a bunch of arguments to just add another
class, so I'm not too excited about finishing it. I would like to hear
critics and suggestions from you.

Oh, I almost forgot: this patch has parts of a larger patch written by
Nicholas Webb which adds a new monster, a satyr (more details in the
documentation). Thanks for the help, Nicholas :-)


